WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:04.440
The finale of "House of the Dragon"&nbsp;
masterfully echoed the show's earliest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:09.122
episodes, while also improving one of the most&nbsp;
impressive sets in "Game of Thrones" history.

00:00:09.122 --> 00:00:13.440
Throughout Season 1, the Painted Table of&nbsp;
Dragonstone has made brief but uneventful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:17.940
appearances. This is the very same table&nbsp;
around which Stannis Baratheon and later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:21.761
Daenerys Targaryen draw up their&nbsp;
battle plans in "Game of Thrones."

00:00:21.761 --> 00:00:26.760
"Conquering Westeros would be easy for you.&nbsp;
But you're not here to be Queen of the Ashes."

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.646
Rhaenyra expresses a similar wish in the&nbsp;
finale while standing around the same table.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:34.740
"I do not wish to rule over&nbsp;
a kingdom of ash and bone."

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:39.720
We know from the books that the table — which&nbsp;
doubles as a map of Westeros — was commissioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:45.360
by none other than King Aegon I himself before&nbsp;
his conquest, which is why it's in Dragonstone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:50.340
instead of King's Landing. Many fans considered&nbsp;
it one of the coolest pieces of set design in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:54.600
the screen adaptations of Martin's world — and&nbsp;
that's before we knew it could be illuminated.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:58.500
Apparently, the instruction manual&nbsp;
got lost sometime in between the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.600
Dance of the Dragons and Robert's Rebellion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:04.931
as no one in "Game of Thrones" seemed to know&nbsp;
about the table's awesome light-up feature.

00:01:04.931 --> 00:01:09.540
If you experienced déjà vu when Otto&nbsp;
arrived with his envoy at Dragonstone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:15.240
that was intentional. The Hand of King Aegon&nbsp;
II arrives unannounced to offer Rhaenyra terms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:19.620
Daemon is there waiting with guards who are&nbsp;
loyal to the Blacks, but Otto says he's under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:24.060
strict instructions to only deliver Queen&nbsp;
Alicent's message to "Princess" Rhaenyra.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:27.720
The Black Queen flies in on the back&nbsp;
of Syrax and lands on the bridge behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:31.920
the Green party. She dismounts, then&nbsp;
positions herself in between Daemon and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:35.040
Otto. The two men are hoping&nbsp;
for war, Daemon outwardly so.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.900
Otto is clearly hoping for an escalation on&nbsp;
the Rogue Prince's part so he has justification&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:44.400
to kill them all and stymy any challenge to&nbsp;
his grandson. It's left to Rhaenyra, again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:48.840
to force cooler heads to prevail. She's&nbsp;
angry enough that her birthright has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:53.160
stolen to toss Otto's pin into the water&nbsp;
below, but after he shows her Alicent's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:57.240
"message," she's steady enough to tell him&nbsp;
she'll give her an answer the following day.

00:01:57.934 --> 00:02:00.812
"King's Landing will have&nbsp;
my answer on the morrow."

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:06.060
This scene is a callback to "House of the Dragon"&nbsp;
Episode 2, in which Otto sails to Dragonstone to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:10.380
confront Daemon about his seizure of what's&nbsp;
supposed to be the heir's seat. Back then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:15.120
a teenage Rhaenyra landed Syrax in almost&nbsp;
exactly the same spot and walked to almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:20.160
exactly the same place between who was then just&nbsp;
her uncle and her father's "vulture" of a Hand.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:25.080
But during that debacle, her back was turned to&nbsp;
Otto while she dealt with Daemon. In Episode 10,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:30.000
her back is to her husband as she tries to&nbsp;
outwit the engineer of the coup against her.

00:02:30.234 --> 00:02:35.040
Alicent Hightower doesn't show up in Episode&nbsp;
10, but it's clear she thinks she has an ace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:39.900
up her sleeve. Before Rhaenyra gives Otto her&nbsp;
non-answer, he hands her a folded-up piece of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:44.220
paper and lets her know that the Queen has not&nbsp;
forgotten the love they once had for each other.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:50.400
As her envoy, Otto insists that Alicent wants&nbsp;
to avoid bloodshed and broker peace. Rhaenyra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:54.840
opens up what viewers might have expected to&nbsp;
be a hand-written message. Instead, we see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:59.460
an illustrated page from a history textbook.&nbsp;
This is the page that a very young Rhaenyra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:03.780
tore out of the book when she was supposed to&nbsp;
be studying with Alicent at the Septa's behest.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:09.300
Alicent's token of affection is also shrewd&nbsp;
statecraft. That she kept the page is meant to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:13.680
show her friend-turned-enemy that she has been&nbsp;
pining for what they once had all these years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:16.380
but she's also hoping the ploy&nbsp;
works in the Greens' favor.

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:21.420
It's significant that the entry is about&nbsp;
Nymeria, a woman who ruled alongside her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:25.560
husband and who brought her people together.&nbsp;
It's also symbolic that, in their youth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:29.940
a willful Rhaenyra ripped out the page&nbsp;
and gave it to an obedient Alicent, while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:35.418
as mothers, a bolder Alicent returns the page to&nbsp;
a Rhaenyra who's worried about duty and honor.

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:39.600
In discussing their path forward, Queen&nbsp;
Rhaenyra tells the Black Council that she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:42.960
wants to know who her allies are before&nbsp;
she sends people to war in her name.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:47.820
Her loyalists assure her some houses, like&nbsp;
Celtigar, can be depended upon for support,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:52.440
but the fealty of houses who swore oaths&nbsp;
long ago will have to be reaffirmed.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:55.140
Chief among these are Houses Tully, Arryn, Stark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:59.400
and Baratheon. We know how that last one&nbsp;
panned out, but the Blacks' efforts to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:03.300
secure alliances with the other three will&nbsp;
likely be the order of business in Season 2.

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:08.040
That means we'll probably be meeting the&nbsp;
ancestors of the Tullys, Starks, and Arryns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:12.300
and visiting these iconic "Game of Thrones"&nbsp;
locations. Technically, it'll be the show's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:17.715
second visit to the Vale, as we did get a brief&nbsp;
stopover there when Daemon knocked off Rhea Royce.

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:22.740
We can't say if the dragon fight happens in&nbsp;
the show as it does in "Fire &amp; Blood." It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:26.940
a stormy night in both versions, which makes it&nbsp;
hard for onlookers to see in written accounts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:31.740
What everyone agrees upon is that Aemond didn't&nbsp;
fly to Storm's End with the intent to kill Luke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:36.360
and that all anyone saw was two dragons&nbsp;
tangling in a fury of fire and lightning.

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:40.500
"House of the Dragon" renders Aemond's&nbsp;
kin-slaying of Luke as something closer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:43.680
to manslaughter than murder. When they&nbsp;
jump on the backs of their dragons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:47.160
Luke and Aemond both speak to&nbsp;
their mounts in High Valyrian.

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:50.760
Though the one-eyed Prince's evil&nbsp;
laughter and his insistence that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:55.560
Luke owes him a debt suggest otherwise, once&nbsp;
Arrax sprays Vhagar in the face with flames,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.560 --> 00:05:00.540
Aemond tries to put on the brakes. He screams&nbsp;
"Dohaeris!" at Vhagar, who chomps down on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:05.889
poor Luke and Arrax anyway. The word "Dohaeris"&nbsp;
might sound familiar to "Game of Thrones" fans.

00:05:07.249 --> 00:05:08.348
"Valar morghulis."

00:05:08.906 --> 00:05:11.058
"Valar dohaeris."

00:05:11.058 --> 00:05:16.260
"Valar Dohaeris" is a common phrase used&nbsp;
in Essos which translates to "all men must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:20.700
serve," often said in conjunction with&nbsp;
"valar morghulis", which means "all men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:24.720
must die." It's also the title of the first&nbsp;
episode of Season 3 of "Game of Thrones."

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:28.380
For the purposes of "House of the&nbsp;
Dragon," that two royal boys can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:31.920
get their dragons to "dohaeris"&nbsp;
indicates Viserys was right.

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:35.160
"They're a power men never&nbsp;
should have trifled with."

